        <div class="rec-num">1</div>
        <div class="rec-body">
          <h4>Anchor MCQ preparation to exactly 15 questions <span class="priority-tag priority-high">High Priority</span></h4>
          <p>MCQs have been exactly 15 in every year without exception (150/150 consistent). Students should practice 15-question timed MCQ sets. Conceptually, MCQs test: operator syntax, data type memory sizes, format specifiers, loop behaviour, database terminology, and MS-Access object functions — all factual recall that rewards regular revision cards over extended practice.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="rec-item">
        <div class="rec-num">2</div>
        <div class="rec-body">
          <h4>Prepare for 15 Short Questions (current standard since 2021) <span class="priority-tag priority-high">High Priority</span></h4>
          <p>The 2021–2025 format of 15 Short Questions has been the consistent standard for 5 consecutive years. Students sitting 2026 exams should prepare for 15 Short Questions. The earlier 18-question format (2017–2019) is unlikely to return but educators should note the historical range of 9–18 if supplementary papers differ.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="rec-item">
        <div class="rec-num">3</div>
        <div class="rec-body">
          <h4>Dedicate equal depth to C Programming AND DBMS/MS-Access <span class="priority-tag priority-high">High Priority</span></h4>
          <p>Topic analysis shows near-equal distribution between C language questions and DBMS questions across all 10 years. Neither domain can be de-emphasised. Long Questions (3 per year) consistently mix both — e.g., sub-part (a) on loops, sub-part (b) on data types, sub-part (c) on MS-Access objects.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="rec-item">
        <div class="rec-num">4</div>
        <div class="rec-body">
          <h4>Focus curriculum depth on the 5 highest-frequency topic clusters <span class="priority-tag priority-high">High Priority</span></h4>
          <p>Data/DBMS concepts (93 Qs), C preprocessor &amp; file I/O (64 Qs), I/O &amp; format specifiers (54 Qs), MS-Access objects (53 Qs), and Functions in C (41–53 Qs) collectively appear across the largest fraction of all 327 questions. These clusters should receive the deepest curriculum coverage and the most exam practice time.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="rec-item">
        <div class="rec-num">5</div>
        <div class="rec-body">
          <h4>Update curriculum to include post-2022 advanced topics <span class="priority-tag priority-med">Medium Priority</span></h4>
          <p>From 2022 onwards, the exam introduced arrays, recursion, normalization, ER diagrams, and from 2025: pointers, structures/unions, bitwise operators, ACID properties, views, indexes, binary search, and storage classes. Educators should confirm these appear in the syllabus and ensure teaching time is allocated — they are now exam-tested concepts, not peripheral extras.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="rec-item">
        <div class="rec-num">6</div>
        <div class="rec-body">
          <h4>Deprioritise Algorithms &amp; Flowcharts in exam-focused revision <span class="priority-tag priority-low">Low Priority</span></h4>
          <p>Only 3 questions across 10 years mention flowcharts or algorithms. While conceptually foundational, these topics appear to have been removed from active examination. Students pressed for revision time can de-prioritise them for exam purposes (though not for conceptual understanding).</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="rec-item">
        <div class="rec-num">7</div>
        <div class="rec-body">
          <h4>Practice code output-prediction questions as a distinct skill <span class="priority-tag priority-med">Medium Priority</span></h4>
          <p>Multiple Short Questions each year ask "what is the output of this code?" — testing increment/decrement operators, loop counters, and conditional logic. These require a specific dry-run mental execution skill. From 2021 onward, these appear consistently in the Short Question section and benefit from dedicated practice sessions separate from concept explanation.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="rec-item">
        <div class="rec-num">8</div>
        <div class="rec-body">
          <h4>Familiarise with full acronym lists (SQL sub-languages) <span class="priority-tag priority-med">Medium Priority</span></h4>
          <p>SQL-related short questions (write the full form of DDL, DML, DCL, TCL, DBA, RDBMS, DBMS, IDE) have appeared across most years in both MCQ and Short formats. These are high-yield, low-effort items. A one-page acronym reference covering all database-related abbreviations provides disproportionate exam value.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
