      <div class="insight-body">The exam has been structurally stable with MCQs at 15 and Long Questions at 3 throughout. The only structural change was Short Questions: 9 (2015–16) → 18 (2017–19) → 15 (2021–25). Since 2021, the format has been locked at 33 total questions.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="insight-card highlight">
      <div class="insight-title">Finding 2: Dual-Domain Exam</div>
      <div class="insight-body">The exam consistently and equally assesses C programming fundamentals and database management systems (DBMS / MS-Access). Curriculum planners should maintain equal teaching hours for both domains. No evidence of one domain crowding out the other.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="insight-card warning">
      <div class="insight-title">Finding 3: Curriculum Advancement Post-2022</div>
      <div class="insight-body">Starting 2022 and accelerating through 2025, the exam tests progressively more advanced topics — normalization, ER diagrams, ACID properties, pointers, structures, binary search, file handling. This represents a clear curriculum advancement trend educators must track.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="insight-card success">
      <div class="insight-title">Finding 4: High Predictability for Exam Prep</div>
      <div class="insight-body">The exam's structural consistency makes preparation highly predictable. The 5-year stable format (2021–2025) means educators can confidently model mock papers with 15 MCQs + 15 Short + 3 Long, reflecting actual exam expectations with high fidelity.</div>
